        "text": "1. Model Identity\nInvariant: The service must bind a specific model checkpoint hash to every active inference session to ensure deterministic behavior.\nFailure Mode: A background model reload or version mismatch causes the service to serve responses from an unintended or stale model variant.\nVerification: Execute a unit test that initializes the service with a specific checkpoint, generates a fixed prompt, and asserts that the output token sequence matches the expected golden file byte-for-byte.\n\n2. Resource Admission\nInvariant: The system must enforce a strict concurrency limit on active generation requests to prevent memory exhaustion on the GPU.\nFailure Mode: A burst of concurrent requests exceeds the available VRAM, causing out-of-memory errors that crash the inference engine or degrade latency for all users.\nVerification: Run a load test that submits requests equal to the configured concurrency limit plus one, and verify that the excess request is rejected with a 503 Service Unavailable status while existing requests complete successfully.\n\n3. Streaming\nInvariant: Each token chunk must be delivered to the client in the exact order it was generated by the model to maintain semantic coherence.\nFailure Mode: Network jitter or internal queueing delays cause token chunks to arrive out of order, resulting in garbled or nonsensical text for the end user.\nVerification: Implement a client-side integration test that records the sequence of received tokens and asserts that the sequence matches the server-side generation log exactly, including timestamps within a defined tolerance.\n\n4. Cancellation\nInvariant: Upon receiving a cancellation signal, the service must immediately stop generating new tokens and release all associated memory resources.\nFailure Mode: The cancellation signal is ignored or delayed, causing the model to continue consuming compute resources and memory for a request that the user has already abandoned.\nVerification: Initiate a long-running generation request, send a cancellation signal after the first token, and monitor the server logs to confirm that the generation loop terminates within a specified millisecond threshold and that memory usage returns to baseline.\n\n5. Shutdown\nInvariant: The service must drain all active requests gracefully before terminating the process to prevent data loss or client errors.\nFailure Mode: An immediate process kill during active inference causes clients to receive truncated responses or connection reset errors, violating the service level agreement.\nVerification: Trigger a graceful shutdown command while a batch of requests is in progress, and verify that all active requests complete their generation and flush their final tokens before the process exits with a zero status code.",
